course: dictionary
lesson: 10580
title: Underachieve: doing less than you can
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Underachieve is a verb, an action word. To underachieve means to do less well than you are able to, especially at school or work. The word is made of "under" and "achieve", so it describes achieving less than your best.
explain: Context gives you the clues. «He is underachieving in maths.» A teacher writing this believes the boy could do better with more help, effort or interest. The sentence does not say he is bad at maths, only that he is not reaching his level.
explain: The example says it clearly: "Capable of more" means the pupil is underachieving, doing less well than he or she could. Capable means able, so a capable pupil who is not using that ability is underachieving. Try the check: swap in "doing less well than he could" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: The person who underachieves is an underachiever. The opposite of underachieve is overachieve. When you meet a new word in a school report, look at the words around it and ask what the writer thinks the person could do.
fact: Underachieve is a verb: underachieve, underachieves, underachieved, underachieving.
fact: «Underachieving» means doing less well than you are able to.
fact: A person who underachieves is called an underachiever.
fact: The opposite of underachieve is overachieve.
fact: Teachers and parents use this word when they think someone could do better with more help, effort or interest.
exercise: «He is underachieving in maths.» What does underachieving mean here? | Finishing his maths test early | *Doing less well than he is able to | Doing maths faster than everyone else | Being the best at maths in the class
exercise: What is the opposite of underachieve? | Forget | Underestimate | Repeat | *Overachieve
exercise: The example says «Capable of more» means the pupil is underachieving. What does capable of more suggest? | The pupil is too clever for the lesson | *The pupil could do better than they are doing | The pupil is already doing their very best | The pupil is not able to try at all
test: Which word names a person who underachieves? | underground | teacher | achiever | *underachiever
test: Choose the right word: «Sam is ___ in spelling, because he could do much better.» | underestimating | uncertain | underground | *underachieving
exam: Mrs Lee writes, «Tom is underachieving; he is capable of much more.» What does this tell us? | *Tom is doing less well than he could | Tom is doing better than everyone in the class | Tom is not capable of learning at all | Tom has finished all his work early
